  [1mData flow:[0m submit → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m → [38;5;153mlogin[39m → response carries [38;5;153muserId[39m → [38;5;153mlogin[39m persists it via [38;5;153mSession[39m → any later page or
  form reads [38;5;153mSession.getUserId()[39m.

  Two decisions in there worth your explicit sign-off, because I picked them rather than asking:

  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m persists, rather than the submit handler doing it.[0m Login is the thing that establishes identity, so putting
    the write there means no future form can forget it — which is the drift your "other forms will need it later" is
    guarding against. The cost is that [38;5;153mlogin[39m is no longer a pure function; a test has to assert against [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m
    rather than just a return value. The alternative is a pure [38;5;153mlogin[39m with [38;5;153mSession.setUserId(result.userId)[39m in each
    caller. Say if you'd rather have the pure version.
  - [1mFailed logins clear the stored id.[0m The stub always succeeds, so this is dead code today, but leaving it out means a
    real failure later silently keeps the previous user's id in storage.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153msession.js[39m (new), [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (modified), [38;5;153mindex.html[39m (modified). [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are untouched
  — separate Node code with no relationship to this.

  Does that look right so far? Next section is error handling, the security boundary, and testing/tooling.

